ck off the next answer for us  at teams sometimes experience what we  call the slump in the middle where the  project kind of seems to lose momentum  mean this typically is a six-month  project some projects go on for much  longer and so so David can you give us  some ideas on kind of how to keep the  team going during the time we've already  talked about how important it is to  clearly identify what you're going to  try to do and to get a commitment from  the client as to their commitment of  time and if you have those things in  place I think that that that is really  going to help you move through and avoid  a slump I think it's important to keep  timelines short my experience working  with business school alums is that they  can do pretty well for a short period of  time but if you try to do something that  goes nine months or a year year and a  half other things intervene all kinds of  things personal work and so on so what I  like to see is short timelines that are  doable and and being clear on exactly  what the deliverables are for each of  the team members and my sense of this is  that somebody who's not getting the work  done in two weeks is not going to get  the work done if you give them two more  weeks so if you're having somebody who's  having problems keeping up with the  group sit down with them one-on-one find  out what's going on and have a  heart-to-heart on whether or not it's  viable for them to continue I also like  to see overlapping skill sets on teams  so that if somebody is not able to work  at the rate that they thought they could  you have somebody who can pick up things  and that can avoid a slump because you  actually don't have the whole dip  because of one team member  I guess one thing I'd say is uh probably  it's good to have what happens is  sometimes there's a flurry of activity  and then you're waiting for something to  happen you know you may be waiting  oftentimes you end up doing surveys and  it takes some time for people to respond  and for you to understand whether  they're ready to talk to you or to get  responses back as well I've had two  projects where that's happened and  you're sort of saying okay you know  you're kind of waiting for the data  you're not sure what to do and the team  can lose a lot of energy doing that and  I think it's kind of useful to maintain  some contact it's easy to sort of say oh  we'll just wait till we hear back and  then the energy of the team can kind of  die down  - even if it's for a half-an-hour  conference call every week to kind of  keep that up and just say has anyone had  any other thoughts have we done any  secondary research or in any other area  that might have some bearing upon this  you know apart from the surveys or  whatever data we're waiting for  so to find some way to use the time  usefully still I think that can help  amplify your point I think it's  important to continue to have regular  meetings and hopefully in your project  plan you've designed some backup tasks  so that when there is that kind of lull  in the project that there's some you  know research that can still be done or  some interviews that can be conducted  try to overlap those tasks so you don't  get a situation where you're just  waiting for data okay so this is a kind  of a key question I'm going to have  David to kick this one off during the  course of a project sometimes someone  chooses to either drop off the team or  stop showing up for an extended period  of time can you give us an example of  where this happened and what your  suggestions are on dealing with it I can  give you an example of where this  happened it was in the project that we  just finished for the Oakland Zoo in the  middle of the project the Oakland Zoo  hired one of our team members as their  CEO Oh  I never saw access a job placement  agency but it worked for prevents so  what did we do well it's actually kind  of interesting because it was a bit of  one of those things where the the board  consistently has wanted act involved  with the zoo to try to get the executive  director what they don't have the guts  to tell him to do and I think that  you'll find the act teams brought in  innocently in many cases with with  hidden hidden agendas on the part of the  board on the part of the IDI trying to  fire the board or to bring about some  other change that they can't seem to do  more in a more straightforward way in  our case what we did is we stopped  communicating with Vince because we felt  that we were going to be talking to them  about things that might be controversial  and we wanted a chance to talk to the  leadership of the organization first  fortunately what I talked about before  of having some overlapping skills we had  a second team member who was a great  number cruncher and Vincent she had been  doing wonderful analysis of the  historical trends and what would happen  if they kept going the way they were  which as I mentioned before would be out  of business and so she picked up the  pace and she took over and and we  finished off the project now I do hope  that our plan is being instituted in a  way the Oakland Zoo hadn't instituted  our three previous projects for them  because we got our guy there I'm  interested to check back and see how  it's worked out this is probably that's  probably a more unusual situation  situations come up we had a team member  once who who had an accident and you  know there's nothing something can do  you can't do anything about that and  you're out of commission for a month and  what do you do and one of the things  that helps is actually team sizes you  know sometimes it is unmanageable to  have you know that the agendas of  everybody line up when you have 16  members but it's nice for those  situations when they do you know get  pregnant or have to move away or you  know something  that happens when a personal situation  comes up so what I found generally is  that you know what you find is most of  the team members are willing to just  take on a little bit extra in the case  where you have very specialized tasks  then yes you're hoping you have backup  because otherwise you're screwed but you  can always come to a crew and ask her  for another person or you know to get  some extra help I think that's another  thing you can do it's a little tricky  because if it happens midway in the  project there's so much learning that  has gone on that it can be challenging  but that's one place where having a team  more 5-6 members or so does help where  you can take up some of the slack of the  work and often what happens is at least  a couple of times it's happened with me  the team member when they come back are  more than willing to put in an extra  effort towards the end so then the other  people can relax a little bit that's  what tends to happen mostly leader when  someone I've been most familiar with the  the flake someone just doesn't show up  for a couple meetings is for a team  leader to directly address it find out  what's going on and then tell them  everyone else this person's not showing  up because of this because I don't it's  not great for morale when geez you're  waiting 15 minutes is he going to show  this time it's sort of a downer to start  every meeting on is this person going to  be here so just address it yeah and  actually that's something that I  recommend all the project leaders do  especially in the first meeting is find  out you know what people's commitment  really I mean I do they have the stamina  for a four to six month project and kind  of established that right away because  it's different when there's an accident  but often people really have planned  absences and make sure you find out you  know we had a we had a team member who  was studying for the bar so we knew we  were going to lose her assumed weight in  the project we had another situation  where you know we had someone who  probably didn't remember his class year  who was a gray hair 