nd bring all of yourself  when you are in those meetings and it  sounds pretty fundamental but I would  say that every team letter leader if  they had those three things happen it  would go very smoothly actually in terms  of doing stuff you know doing the work  and being coming to the meeting prepared  so if there is a  something to be discussed then you know  make sure you've read the material or  done the work that's required that to  make sure you know we it's really  difficult sometimes to find that one are  in the week or two hours that everybody  can meet from their busy lives and you  don't want to really spend that sort of  updating people and what's happened and  you know be there on time that's the  other thing every ten minute counts you  know if it's if you're meeting around  lunchtime to get your lunch before you  come to the meeting it helps it's little  stuff like that and then the other thing  that can help is actually some some  sense of rotating responsibilities in  terms of you know if we're taking notes  it kind of it's it's easier for  everybody I think if that's on some sort  of a rotating basis so one person is not  always doing the more kind of routine  work of just note-taking setting up  meetings whatever it might be so  sometimes that works and helps as well  with the team clear communication so so  yes the best thing for you to do is to  show up do what you committed to do but  if you can't because you know life  happens make sure you let your team  leader know that you're not going to be  doing what you were assigned and make  sure you let that your your project  leader know as soon as possible  so that your project leader has an  opportunity to to reassign the tasks and  to try to deal with that you know the  recommendation I don't have to project  leaders of course is to build in  redundancy and build some slack in so  that there is that capacity to take over  if somebody has some you know emergency  business meeting they have to fly 